From 4638f977d79762d44b26d1a38a3c2a5b5ab92faf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>
Date: Sat, 1 Aug 2026 14:43:13 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] Harden web browse against DNS-based SSRF bypasses

The previous guard only string-matched the request hostname, so any name that
resolved to a private address (127.0.0.1.nip.io, 169.254.169.254.nip.io) reached
internal services, and a public host could redirect the fetch inward because the
handler followed redirects itself.

SsrfGuard now resolves the host and requires every returned address to be
publicly routable, covering IPv6, IPv4-mapped IPv6, carrier-grade NAT, and
link-local metadata ranges. Redirects are followed manually so each hop is
revalidated, capped at 5 hops. Operators can allow specific private hosts via
Security:Browse:AllowedPrivateHosts for intranet sources.
